      "id": 1,
      "text": "Waterbury earned the nickname 'Brass City' after 19th‑century factories harnessed the Mad and Naugatuck Rivers to mass‑produce brass.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        10
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waterbury%2C_Connecticut",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Immigrant workers from Europe, Asia, and the Caribbean flocked to Waterbury’s brass mills, creating a mosaic of cultures that still echoes today.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        11
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waterbury%2C_Connecticut",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The Italian community shaped Waterbury’s Town Plot, Brooklyn, and North End neighborhoods, leaving lasting culinary and cultural traditions.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        12
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Wikipedia"
      ],
      "source": "https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/Waterbury%2C_Connecticut",
      "category": "culture",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "At Fascia’s Chocolates, a family‑owned factory, visitors can tour, taste, and even craft their own chocolate bar—a sweet slice of local industry.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        15
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://ctvisit.com/listings/city-waterbury",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "The historic Palace Theater, opened in 1922, continues to host Broadway hits like Jersey Boys and Rent, keeping Waterbury’s performing‑arts scene alive.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        24
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://ctvisit.com/listings/city-waterbury",
      "category": "landmark",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Waterbury’s Union Station, built in 1909, once served the New York, New Haven and Hartford Railroad and now houses the Republican‑American newspaper headquarters.",
